// add-kyc-ui.mjs
//
// The screens for tiers and limits from 027 and 028.
//
// WHAT IT ADDS
//
//   /limits        your tier, what you have used today and this month, how
//                  much room is left in your wallet, and the form to move up
//   /admin Identity  the review queue: approve, ask for more, reject
//   supabaseClient  turns limit codes into sentences
//
// THE ERROR MESSAGES
//
// The database raises machine codes — LIMIT_DAILY:40.00000000:minimum — so it
// stays language-neutral and the numbers survive. Left alone, a customer sees
// that string. rpc() now rewrites them:
//
//   "That is over today's limit — 40 KPC left. Verify your account to raise it."
//
// Done in the client wrapper for the same reason the PIN is: every page gets
// it without knowing limits exist.
//
// THE USAGE BARS COME FIRST ON /limits
//
// Somebody opening that page is usually there because something was just
// refused. The answer is in the three bars, so they are above the upgrade
// pitch rather than below it.
//
// SOURCE OF FUNDS IS OPTIONAL ON THE FORM
//
// It is a real AML requirement and a terrible first question. A reviewer asks
// for it with "More info" when the amounts warrant it. The admin card shows
// "not given" rather than an empty row, so a reviewer knows it was not asked
// rather than wondering if it failed to save.
//
// REQUIRES migrations 027_kyc_limits.sql and 028_tiers_v2.sql.

